# Logitech G915 X keyboards on their cable, vendor HID++ interface ->
# uaccess for the seat user: the wired G915 X (046d:c359), the G915 X
# LIGHTSPEED (046d:c356) and the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL (046d:c357); and the
# two LIGHTSPEED models' nodes of their own behind their LIGHTSPEED receiver
# (40B5 and 40B6, below), which carry what is typed on them too. The ids are
# from Logitech G HUB's device database and Solaar's listings of the first two
# (issues 3113 and 3137), read 2026-10-21. The G915 X LIGHTSPEED's were
# checked on one on 2026-10-23; the other two are not yet checked on this
# hardware.
#
# Interface 02, the vendor collection alone, as on the wired G515 TKL, whose
# feature table the G915 X's matches index for index: so on the G915 X
# LIGHTSPEED, read 2026-10-23, and taken to be so on the other two. A
# keyboard's other interfaces carry its keystrokes, and read access there
# would be a keylogger surface. gearbench-ctl report gives each node's
# interface and whether its report descriptor holds HID++, read from sysfs,
# so it needs no access to the node. Gearbench opens interface 02 alone, so
# if HID++ is on another, put a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/
# without that model's line: it overrides this file by name, and a reinstall
# or upgrade leaves it alone. Then run sudo udevadm control --reload, replug
# the keyboard, and paste the report's block into a device report.
#
# Once a host writes 0x8051 fn 3 `03 ff`, as Gearbench does at each setup to
# light the FN keys, interface 02 carries the modifier keys held (Ctrl,
# Shift, Alt, Super and FN, each press and release), as the wired G515 TKL's
# does (70-g515.rules) and the G915 X LIGHTSPEED's did on 2026-10-23, until
# the keyboard loses power: unplugged, where it has no battery, or switched
# off, since the G915 X LIGHTSPEED kept them through a replug of its cable.
# The other two are taken to do the same. Gearbench
# reads FN alone. Any program of the seat's user can write that and read
# them, and one that holds the node open reads another account's after a
# user switch; 70-g515.rules says how to limit the node to a group's
# accounts.
#
# Matched on properties, as 70-g915.rules is, for the reason it gives.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c359", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c356", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c357", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"

# The G915 X LIGHTSPEED and the G915 X LIGHTSPEED TKL through their
# LIGHTSPEED receiver: the hidraw node of its own, 0003:046D:40B5.<n> and
# 0003:046D:40B6.<n>, that the kernel's hid-logitech-dj gives a device paired
# to a receiver it drives, as 70-g515.rules says of the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's
# 40B4 node. The 40b5 and 40b6 ids are from G HUB's device records, and
# Solaar issue 3328's listing names 40B5 on slot 1, read 2026-10-22. Neither
# names the receiver: G HUB gives its kind, LS2, the kind it gives the G515
# LIGHTSPEED TKL, whose receiver is a c547 (70-g515.rules). The G915 X
# LIGHTSPEED's was a c547, with 40B5 on slot 1, on 2026-10-23; the TKL's is
# not yet checked on this hardware, and a c547 is assumed.
#
# This node carries what is typed on the keyboard as well as its HID++
# traffic, as the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's does (70-g515.rules says why). So
# with these lines installed, any program the user of the active seat
# session runs can read what is typed on the G915 X LIGHTSPEED or the G915 X
# LIGHTSPEED TKL, not only Gearbench, which drops those reports unread. The
# owner granted them on 2026-10-22, as the G515 LIGHTSPEED TKL's node was
# granted. Each carries no other device's reports while nothing else is
# paired to its receiver (70-g502x.rules says why).
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the
# keyboard, a password at the login screen included. On a machine several
# people use, that file's group grant, in a copy of this file in
# /etc/udev/rules.d/, limits the node to the group's accounts. A member then
# reads it from any session, whoever has the seat.
#
# Matched by the HID device's name, as 70-g502x.rules matches the mouse: udev
# gives this node the receiver's properties, so a property match would grant
# the receiver's own interfaces too. KERNELS matches a parent's kernel name,
# which for the paired device is bus:vendor:product.instance.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:40B5.*", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:40B6.*", TAG+="uaccess"
